! Write a function that finds all the roots of any polynomial of degree 1
! and higher. The roots may be real or complex or duplicate or zero. A
! polynomial of degree n has always n roots, including duplicates. Input
! checking and error estimate display is not asked for, but check your
! roots by evaluating the polynomial.
! 
! Wikipedia is rather long, but gives at the end links to a bunch of root
! solving algorithms. Some of them are specific for polynomials. Select
! one you like, or use a library if available.
! 
! The REXX entry has many examples taken from Roots of a quadratic
! function, Roots of a cubic polynomial and Roots of a quartic polynomial
! so you may compare the roots found. That's why the entry is not
! optimised for degree 1-4 (by calling the closed formula solutions).
! 
! Extra kudos for the last example: Conway's constant is the only real
! positive root of an 71-degree polynomial.


